Give the PHY time to negotiate, and retry DHCP.

The BIOS runs within milliseconds of FPGA configuration, but gigabit
autonegotiation takes 1-3 seconds. `netboot()` sends ONE DHCP discover and, on
no reply, falls through to the compiled-in addresses -- so the usual outcome at
power-on is a card that asks before the link exists, boots nowhere, and sits
silent. There is no retry anywhere in that path.

Upstream: enjoy-digital/litex#2603, "BIOS netboot() has no PHY link wait or
retry (fails at power-on)".

This matters most on a card with nothing in flash to fall back to: netboot is
then the ONLY boot medium, so one unlucky DHCP attempt is the difference
between a working panel and a dead one. Retrying costs nothing on a card that
already has link -- the first attempt answers and the loop exits.

--- a/litex/soc/software/bios/boot.c
+++ b/litex/soc/software/bios/boot.c
@@ -625,7 +625,23 @@
 	 * every card, including ones that will boot from flash anyway. */
 	{
 		struct udp_dhcp_lease lease;
-		if(udp_dhcp_request(macadr, &lease)) {
+		int got = 0, tries;
+		/* Gigabit autonegotiation takes 1-3 s and the BIOS runs within
+		 * milliseconds of configuration, so the FIRST discover routinely
+		 * goes out before the link exists. One attempt with no retry
+		 * then means a card that boots nowhere and sits silent -- fatal
+		 * on a card with nothing in flash to fall back to, where netboot
+		 * is the only boot medium. See enjoy-digital/litex#2603.
+		 * A card that already has link answers on the first pass and
+		 * never waits. */
+		for(tries = 0; tries < 8 && !got; tries++) {
+			if(tries)
+				busy_wait(800);
+			got = udp_dhcp_request(macadr, &lease);
+		}
+		if(got) {
+			if(tries > 1)
+				printf("DHCP: answered on attempt %d\n", tries);
 			if(lease.local_ip) {
 				local_ip[0] = (lease.local_ip >> 24) & 0xff;
 				local_ip[1] = (lease.local_ip >> 16) & 0xff;
